WEBVTT

00:00.150 --> 00:01.740
Instructor: Often when we're using "git pull,"

00:01.740 --> 00:04.290
we can get away with an even shorter syntax

00:04.290 --> 00:06.810
where we don't need to specify the remote

00:06.810 --> 00:08.910
or the branch we want to pull.

00:08.910 --> 00:12.180
Because it's really common to be on some branch, right?

00:12.180 --> 00:15.090
I'm on the food branch,

00:15.090 --> 00:18.570
and if I want the latest from "origin/food,"

00:18.570 --> 00:20.730
that's where I would want to get changes from.

00:20.730 --> 00:23.400
Normally I'm not gonna pull some other branch

00:23.400 --> 00:24.780
onto the food branch.

00:24.780 --> 00:27.300
I want the latest from food on GitHub.

00:27.300 --> 00:31.170
So I could do "git pull origin food,"

00:31.170 --> 00:32.190
I'm on the food branch,

00:32.190 --> 00:35.100
I want food changes coming from origin.

00:35.100 --> 00:36.420
We only have one remote right now,

00:36.420 --> 00:38.310
so it should be origin anyway.

00:38.310 --> 00:40.590
Right, we haven't configured another remote.

00:40.590 --> 00:44.203
But "origin" is just such a common name in Git.

00:44.203 --> 00:46.020
Again, you can change all of these,

00:46.020 --> 00:49.237
but it's so common that it will default.

00:49.237 --> 00:51.300
"git pull" will default to "origin"

00:51.300 --> 00:53.580
if you don't specify a remote.

00:53.580 --> 00:56.730
And the branch will automatically default

00:56.730 --> 00:59.070
to whatever tracking connection is configured

00:59.070 --> 01:00.690
for your current branch.

01:00.690 --> 01:03.870
And, as in every example we've done so far,

01:03.870 --> 01:06.060
the tracking connections are set up for us.

01:06.060 --> 01:08.160
We never specify a tracking connection,

01:08.160 --> 01:09.480
I didn't create them,

01:09.480 --> 01:11.880
simply by switching to a remote branch.

01:11.880 --> 01:14.730
If you recall, I go way back here,

01:14.730 --> 01:16.500
when we did a "git switch,"

01:16.500 --> 01:18.990
almost there, almost there.

01:18.990 --> 01:21.840
Okay, when we did this, right, "git switch puppies,"

01:21.840 --> 01:24.780
or "git switch food," or "git switch movies,"

01:24.780 --> 01:26.850
that branch was set up for us,

01:26.850 --> 01:29.640
and it automatically had a tracking reference

01:29.640 --> 01:31.890
to the remote branch.

01:31.890 --> 01:34.807
So typically these are just the same name.

01:34.807 --> 01:36.307
"puppies," "origin/puppies,"

01:36.307 --> 01:39.480
"food," "origin/food," "main," "origin/main."

01:39.480 --> 01:42.390
You absolutely can configure them differently.

01:42.390 --> 01:46.249
I could have my food branch, my local food branch,

01:46.249 --> 01:51.249
for whatever reason, track the movies branch on origin.

01:51.390 --> 01:53.550
You just normally wouldn't want that,

01:53.550 --> 01:55.320
you normally don't do that.

01:55.320 --> 01:58.890
So "git pull" depends on the context of where you are.

01:58.890 --> 02:02.040
If I'm on the master branch and I run "git pull,"

02:02.040 --> 02:05.970
Git will automatically default to "origin" as the remote,

02:05.970 --> 02:08.640
and "master" because I'm on the master branch,

02:08.640 --> 02:10.710
and currently the tracking branch,

02:10.710 --> 02:13.260
the default value is "origin/master."

02:13.260 --> 02:14.790
So same thing with puppies.

02:14.790 --> 02:17.970
If I'm on the puppies branch and I run "git pull,"

02:17.970 --> 02:20.737
it's just a way of saying, "Please get the latest changes

02:20.737 --> 02:22.957
"and pull them down, merge them into this branch,

02:22.957 --> 02:24.697
"from the remote version,

02:24.697 --> 02:27.420
"from the origin version of puppies."

02:27.420 --> 02:28.710
So it's really common to do this,

02:28.710 --> 02:31.710
because that's normally where you want to pull changes from.

02:31.710 --> 02:34.380
Right, if I'm on the food branch, I want origin/food.

02:34.380 --> 02:35.280
So let's do it.

02:35.280 --> 02:39.030
I updated the food branch in between videos

02:39.030 --> 02:40.350
just to make it faster.

02:40.350 --> 02:42.000
I didn't want you to have to watch.

02:42.000 --> 02:44.850
I added a tea file, all right?

02:44.850 --> 02:47.760
I don't have that locally, I'll just prove it.

02:47.760 --> 02:48.720
Right, we don't see tea.

02:48.720 --> 02:50.250
We have coffee, but no tea.

02:50.250 --> 02:54.540
If I do "git pull," it pulled the food branch from origin,

02:54.540 --> 02:57.390
I see the tea file right there.

02:57.390 --> 02:58.860
I can view it.

02:58.860 --> 03:02.280
If I do "git log," I have that new commit.

03:02.280 --> 03:04.080
One more example.

03:04.080 --> 03:06.990
I updated the fantasy branch.

03:06.990 --> 03:10.680
I added a new file, "gryphon," there it is.

03:10.680 --> 03:11.513
I don't have that.

03:11.513 --> 03:16.430
If I switch to fantasy, "git switch fantasy," okay?

03:19.680 --> 03:22.350
I don't have that new gryphon file.

03:22.350 --> 03:25.830
You can see over here, it doesn't show up.

03:25.830 --> 03:29.160
I could do "git pull origin fantasy,"

03:29.160 --> 03:31.857
or just rely on the default, "git pull."

03:33.060 --> 03:33.893
And now I have it.

03:33.893 --> 03:37.380
It shows up there, and here it is.

03:37.380 --> 03:39.810
So if you want, you can use that syntax of,

03:39.810 --> 03:42.510
you know, typing out "origin" and typing the branch name.

03:42.510 --> 03:44.610
Sometimes you need to use that syntax.

03:44.610 --> 03:47.640
For example, if you have multiple remotes, which we will,

03:47.640 --> 03:49.830
we'll have an upstream and an origin.

03:49.830 --> 03:53.280
So sometimes we don't want to default to that origin.

03:53.280 --> 03:56.250
But for now, really most of the time

03:56.250 --> 03:57.630
you can just use "git pull,"

03:57.630 --> 04:00.090
assuming you wanna update the puppies branch

04:00.090 --> 04:01.950
with the changes from origin/puppies,

04:01.950 --> 04:05.100
or the movies branch with the changes from origin/movies.

04:05.100 --> 04:08.340
All right, so that about wraps up fetching and pulling.

04:08.340 --> 04:10.560
Hopefully this diagram makes more sense now.

04:10.560 --> 04:13.890
When we "fetch," we're getting changes from a remote repo,

04:13.890 --> 04:16.140
but they're not integrated into our workspace.

04:16.140 --> 04:19.020
When we "pull," we get those changes from the remote repo,

04:19.020 --> 04:22.620
and then they are merged into our current HEAD branch.

04:22.620 --> 04:25.200
Where we pull from matters.

04:25.200 --> 04:27.150
And that brings me to the last slide.

04:27.150 --> 04:30.960
Fetch gets changes from a remote branch, or branches.

04:30.960 --> 04:33.360
Updates the remote tracking branches with the changes,

04:33.360 --> 04:36.480
does not merge anything into your current HEAD branch.

04:36.480 --> 04:38.070
And it's safe to do at anytime.

04:38.070 --> 04:40.867
It's not gonna screw up any work that you're doing.

04:40.867 --> 04:42.810
"git pull," on the other hand,

04:42.810 --> 04:45.210
also gets changes from remote branches,

04:45.210 --> 04:48.000
but it updates the current branch with the new changes,

04:48.000 --> 04:48.990
merging them in.

04:48.990 --> 04:51.120
And it can result in merge conflicts.

04:51.120 --> 04:53.340
It is not safe to do at any time,

04:53.340 --> 04:55.920
meaning that, you know, you can have conflicts,

04:55.920 --> 04:59.700
you can have changes integrated into your working directory,

04:59.700 --> 05:02.790
conflicting with work that you had been working on.

05:02.790 --> 05:06.180
So not recommended to do if you have uncommitted work.

05:06.180 --> 05:08.493
All right, so that's fetch and pull.
